WEBVTT - Module 3: Home Fire Escape Plan

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Welcome to the explainer! Today we're doing more than just learning something new.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.500
We're actually going to build something together: a life-saving fire escape plan for your home!

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.500
Trust me, this is the kind of stuff that can make all the difference when every single second counts.

4
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.000
To really prepare for a fire, you've got to understand what you're up against. These four words say it all:

5
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.000
FAST, DARK, HOT, and DEADLY!

6
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:33.000
Forget what you see in the movies. In a real fire, smoke is so thick you literally can't see your own hand!

7
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.500
Getting that picture in your head is the first real step to staying safe.

8
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
And this number: 30 seconds. That's how fast a fire can get out of control!

9
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:50.000
In less time than it takes to scroll through your phone, a little flame can turn into a massive, life-threatening blaze.

10
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.000
There is absolutely zero time to waste!

11
00:00:54.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Here's a fact that surprises a lot of people: it's usually not the flames that pose the biggest immediate danger.

12
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:06.500
It's all those toxic gases! They can knock you out or get you completely disoriented in just a few seconds.

13
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.000
And that's why having a plan you've actually practiced isn't just a smart idea — it's essential for survival.

14
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.000
Okay, so with that reality check out of the way, let's get to work building your escape plan piece by piece!

15
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:26.000
We're going to create a solid blueprint for safety that everyone in your house, from kids to adults, can follow.

16
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:31.000
The whole foundation of your plan really comes down to two simple things:

17
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.500
First, grab a piece of paper and sketch out your home's floor plan. Just show the rooms.

18
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.500
Then — and this is the big one — for every single room, you need to find TWO WAYS OUT!

19
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Your primary route is the door you use every single day. That's always your first choice, as long as it's clear.

20
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.500
But what if that door is blocked by fire or smoke? That's where your backup plan kicks in: your secondary route.

21
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Which is usually a window, and becomes your lifeline! Make sure those windows actually open easily.

22
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:07.500
And hey, if you've got rooms on a second story, you should have an escape ladder handy.

23
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.500
All right, a blueprint on paper is just wallpaper until you put it into action!

24
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.500
Knowing the plan is one thing, but actually being able to do it under pressure is a whole different ballgame.

25
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:26.500
The second you hear that smoke alarm, it means one thing: GO!

26
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.500
Not go see what's happening, not grab your wallet — it means GET OUT right now!

27
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:38.500
Here's a bit of fire science that can save your life: heat rises, and so does all that thick, toxic smoke.

28
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.500
The cleanest air you can actually breathe is going to be down near the floor.

29
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
So get down on your hands and knees and crawl! Don't walk — stay low and go!

30
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:56.500
Now, before you even think about grabbing that doorknob, you have to stop and ask one critical question.

31
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Busting through a door without checking could lead you straight into a wall of fire!

32
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:08.000
How do you check? Use the BACK OF YOUR HAND — it's way more sensitive to heat than your palm.

33
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:14.500
Gently feel the doorknob and the cracks around the door. If you feel ANY warmth at all, that door stays shut!

34
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.500
It means there's fire on the other side. Immediately turn around and use your second way out.

35
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.000
If everything feels cool, lean your shoulder against the door and crack it open just a tiny bit.

36
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:33.000
If you see smoke or feel heat, slam it shut! If it looks clear, make your escape.

37
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:38.500
Okay, so you're out of the house. Amazing! But the plan isn't over yet.

38
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:45.000
The next crucial part is making sure everyone is accounted for. A designated meeting spot is non-negotiable!

39
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:51.500
It's the only way you quickly know everyone is safe, and prevents anyone from running back inside.

40
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.500
Pick something permanent: a big tree, a streetlight, or your neighbor's mailbox.

41
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:04.500
And this is the most important rule of all: ONCE YOU ARE OUT, YOU STAY OUT!

42
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.500
Never ever go back into a burning building — not for a phone, photos, or even a pet!

43
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.500
Let the professionals with the right gear and training take it from there.

44
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.500
A plan you don't practice is just a wish. Turn your plan into an instinct!

45
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Run a full-on fire drill with everybody in the house at least TWICE a year!

46
00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:36.000
Practice builds muscle memory. It replaces panic with a life-saving reflex on autopilot.

47
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:42.500
So right now, after this is over, take 5 minutes and talk to your family.

48
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Draw that map, pick your meeting spot, and set a date for your first drill!
